WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.395
This is a take-home case I wrote for a TikTok live-streaming role.

00:00:04.695 --> 00:00:10.958
The brief gave me five music creators, eighty thousand dollars, and eight weeks until a music festival.

00:00:11.258 --> 00:00:15.570
My answer has two parts. Fix what each streaming hour earns.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:18.454
Then use the festival as a growth engine.

00:00:19.754 --> 00:00:23.279
Before any tactics, I set the scope and the baseline.

00:00:23.579 --> 00:00:29.808
Every target compares the campaign weeks with a seven-day average from before, with outlier days removed.

00:00:30.108 --> 00:00:32.698
The targets are estimates from public research.

00:00:32.998 --> 00:00:35.602
I check them against internal data in week one.

00:00:36.902 --> 00:00:38.381
Here is the diagnosis.

00:00:38.681 --> 00:00:42.275
The two creators who stream the most are declining the fastest.

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:47.222
Two others make forty-eight percent of the revenue in twenty-three percent of the hours.

00:00:47.522 --> 00:00:50.292
So the problem is efficiency, not effort.

00:00:50.592 --> 00:00:54.779
Revenue per hour differs by five times across the five creators.

00:00:56.079 --> 00:00:58.512
So each creator gets a different plan.

00:00:58.812 --> 00:01:02.046
Back the two winners with a fixed schedule and fan events.

00:01:02.346 --> 00:01:08.726
Fix the two who are declining, with fewer, better hours, and new formats that bring in new viewers.

00:01:09.026 --> 00:01:12.547
And hold the one in the middle, before the same pattern sets in.

00:01:13.847 --> 00:01:15.673
The festival does three jobs.

00:01:15.973 --> 00:01:18.590
It recruits musicians who have never streamed.

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:22.496
It gives current creators a competition and a training course.

00:01:22.796 --> 00:01:25.019
And it puts the best on a real stage.

00:01:25.319 --> 00:01:30.456
Most of the budget goes to the stage and the competition, because each makes the other worth more.

00:01:31.756 --> 00:01:35.856
The program is called Road to Pulse. From your room to the main stage.

00:01:36.156 --> 00:01:41.735
A short video counts as an entry, so new musicians can join without any streaming history.

00:01:42.035 --> 00:01:46.513
Weekly rounds lead to semifinals, and a final on the festival stage.

00:01:46.813 --> 00:01:49.415
The prizes are career steps, not cash.

00:01:50.715 --> 00:01:54.332
On site, the festival is a stage and a content factory.

00:01:54.632 --> 00:02:01.007
QR codes link each stage and booth to a creator's stream, so the impact is measurable per creator.

00:02:01.307 --> 00:02:03.009
I split the plan in two.

00:02:03.309 --> 00:02:06.403
The core plan runs on confirmed resources only.

00:02:06.703 --> 00:02:09.881
The stretch plan happens only if partners say yes.

00:02:11.181 --> 00:02:15.272
Delivery runs in five workstreams, with seven decision points.

00:02:15.572 --> 00:02:21.407
Early signals come first: five hundred entries, and one hundred fifty first-time streamers.

00:02:21.707 --> 00:02:25.567
In week four, the numbers decide whether to continue or move the money.

00:02:25.867 --> 00:02:30.296
This is a plan, not a result. Every target gets checked in week one.

00:02:30.596 --> 00:02:31.782
Thank you for watching.

